<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koe-thaung Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1554">1554</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kothaung Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Koe-thaung Temple is a large Buddhist temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a> in Rakhine State, Myanmar, built by King Min Dikkha in the mid-16th century. Its name means 'shrine of the 90,000 images', after the many carved Buddha images lining its galleries. It is the largest temple of Mrauk U and was partly ruined before modern restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koe-thaung-temple.jpg" alt="Koe-thaung Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jmhullot · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KoeThaung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
